00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
from the start of the pandemic former  fda commissioner dr scott gottlieb was  in regular contact with key players in  the trump administration and his new  book uncontrolled spread why covet 19  crushed us and how we can defeat the  pandemic the next pandemic details the  mistakes that u.s officials have made  and may still be making in responding to  the virus dr gottlieb is also a member  of pfizer's board of directors and he's  here for his first live interview about  the new book how covet 19 crushed us and  why  i want to get to the book but because  you are on the board of pfizer and we  have this big pfizer news i want to get  to that first how soon could children  under the age of 12 start receiving  these vaccines so this data was on in  children 5 to 11 years old it's a 10  microgram vaccine so it's effectively  the same vaccine that's being used in  adults but in a lower dose pfizer tested  multiple doses to find the the optimal  dose that had the best compromise  between reducing the number of  vaccine-related side effects while still  providing efficacy that's on par with  what we saw in 16 to 25 year olds so  this is basically one-third of the  dose that's gonna that's already  approved in adults now that they have  the data that they could be ready to  file this within days with the food and  drug administration the fda has said  that the review of this vaccine they've  said publicly is going to be a matter of  weeks not months now having been at the  agency i interpret that to mean four to  six weeks so i think that there's a  possibility you could have a vaccine as  early as around halloween maybe a little  bit later you might lose a week here or  there but i think that if things go well  the fda looks at this data and feels  confident that it demonstrates that this  vaccine is safe and effective for  children five to eleven you could have a  vaccine within a month to six weeks one  of the compelling arguments in the book  here is that our response to pandemics  needs to be treated as a national  security issue with that in mind as we  look at an fda panel declining to  recommend booster shots for all  americans do you think they're moving  quickly enough well look i think what  they did was was appropriate they're  going to take a staged response to  trying to make boosters available to the  american population so what they said  was 65 and above and people who are at  risk of a severe outcome from covert  that could be expanded and maybe even  narrowed by the cdc once the cdc makes a  recommendation based on the fda's advice  but what i think you're going to see is  public health regulators now collect  more information to demonstrate that the  vaccines might be effective in people of  a younger age and they'll start walking  it down the age continuum so i think  that this is a start i think they  started with the population that's most  at risk of breakthrough infections which  is the older population they were  vaccinated a long interval ago and they  also have the worst outcomes if they do  contract coverage so i think it's a  prudent way to do this in a staged  fashion and i can see them now  collecting more data and then walking  this down to age continuum yeah i want  to get i want to follow up on that in  just a second but to go back to the  question about the kids was there  anything about side effects for children  who get the booster  so the profile in 5 to 11 looked on if  on par with what we saw in 16 to 25 year  olds pfizer tested three different doses  in children ages five to eleven to try  to find the optimal dose that provided  the same immune response in terms of the  kind of antibody production that you  were getting with the lowest possible  reactogenicity that's vaccine related  side effects like fever is an injection  site reaction the profile of the vaccine  in 5 to 11 actually looked better than  in 1625 in terms of the antibody  production and the side effect profile  but there was a real emphasis on trying  to find a dose that was optimized  insofar as it provided a sufficient  immune response but also the lowest  possible reactorgenicity and actually  the immune response between the 10  microgram dose and the 20 microgram dose  seemed comparable and the reason the 10  microgram dose was chosen was because it  seemed more tolerable in the young kids  you know the fda panel when you look at  israel israel has said listen everybody  needs to get the booster it's 10 times  more effective and the fda panel isn't  there yet israel seems to be way ahead  of us when it comes to fighting covert  why is that well look they're moving  more quickly they basically got 50  experts in a room to come to a decision  about making boosters available we have  a very iterative process here in the us  where multiple regulatory bodies weigh  in on it it's a slower process and we  also haven't been collecting data  systematically looking at boosters in  this country and also looking at  breakthrough infections the data we're  relying on is the israeli data so i  think that they were in a position to  move more quickly and roll it out more  quickly we'll get there they're the size  of an american state no they're a  country but they're like new jersey no  doubt your book examines what you call  the failures of the cdc what do you  think the cdc got wrong and what do you  think they're still getting wrong well  look i think at the outset they didn't  have the logistical capability to really  roll out a response that was of the  magnitude that was required they didn't  have the capability to handle a national  level public health crisis it's a very  retrospective mindset at the cdc they're  a high science organization that does  deep analytical work they don't have a  logistical capability so they couldn't  scale production of a test and  distribution of a test early on that got  us really behind they couldn't scale  production of a vaccine and actually  roll it out to the public they couldn't  collect information in a real-time  fashion and do analytical work to inform  real-time decision making so for example  for months they were still recommending  that people wipe down their groceries  and had a presumption that a lot of the  spread was through contaminated surfaces  when in fact it was and it was through  aerosols so it's deeply analytical they  would rather spend four months  collecting rigorous data doing  analytical work and come out with a  definitive answer rather than earth  surface real-time  information that can inform current  policy decision making you sound  frustrated by that scott  well it frustrated all of us look there  were a lot of things that felt very  conflicting that guidance on how far  should you stand apart from someone to  reduce your risk is it six feet is it  three feet is it ten feet it kept  changing the initial recommendation put  forward to the white house was actually  for ten feet of distance they eventually  revised it to six feet and subsequently  revised it to three feet we could have  done that a lot earlier that was the  single costliest advice that cdc gave  because it forced many schools to stay  closed because they couldn't provide for  six feet of distance between now listen  get it right you called it arbitrary  which is not a word you want an  association with health guidance uh dr  scott gottlieb who will not be invited  to the cdc holiday party this year but  has a lot of good information here in  the book it's called uncontrolled spread  and it goes on sale tomorrow dr gottlieb  thank you very much thanks a lot thank  you so much eye opening stuff in this  book indeed

